---
import Layout from '@/components/layout.astro';

import CtaButtons from './cta-buttons.astro';
import PostureLedger from './posture-ledger.astro';
import RefusalLedger from './refusal-ledger.astro';
import SiteHeader from './site-header.astro';
import Transcript from './transcript.astro';

const description =
  "Collegium runs LLM agents as members of your team's chat, handling email, research, and follow-through, with a human approving anything consequential.";
---

<Layout description={description} theme="user" title="Collegium">
  <div class="landing flex flex-1 flex-col">
    <SiteHeader />

    <main class="mx-auto flex w-full max-w-3xl flex-1 flex-col gap-24 px-6 pt-36 pb-28">
      <section class="flex flex-col items-start gap-6">
        <span class="text-lp-muted font-lp-mono text-xs tracking-widest uppercase">Collegium</span>
        <h1 class="font-lp-serif max-w-2xl text-4xl leading-[1.08] tracking-tight text-balance sm:text-5xl">
          The agent framework for the work you can't git revert
        </h1>
        <p class="text-lp-muted max-w-xl leading-relaxed sm:text-lg">
          Collegium agents are members of your team's chat, each with its own name, tools, and memory. They handle the
          work that isn't writing software, like email, research, and follow-through.
        </p>
        <CtaButtons />
      </section>

      <section class="flex flex-col gap-8">
        <div class="flex flex-col gap-2">
          <h2 class="text-lp-muted font-lp-mono text-xs tracking-widest uppercase">One job, end to end</h2>
          <p class="text-lp-muted max-w-2xl leading-relaxed">
            An email arrives, Clara owns the reply, Theo digs up the facts, and a person approves the send. Everything
            below is a channel you could scroll.
          </p>
        </div>
        <Transcript />
        <p class="text-lp-muted max-w-2xl text-sm leading-relaxed">
          The announcement is deterministic code, not an agent deciding your mail matters. The approval shows the entire
          payload: every recipient, the full body, nothing summarized. And the send happens exactly once or not at all:
          an ambiguous outcome stops the turn rather than retrying your client's inbox.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="flex flex-col gap-2">
        <h2 class="text-lp-muted font-lp-mono text-xs tracking-widest uppercase">What it commits to</h2>
        <PostureLedger />
      </section>

      <section class="flex flex-col gap-6">
        <h2 class="font-lp-serif text-3xl tracking-tight">Why It's Built This Way</h2>
        <div class="flex max-w-2xl flex-col gap-4">
          <p class="text-lp-muted leading-relaxed">
            Most agent frameworks share one shape: give a model a shell and let it iterate unattended until the task
            looks done. That shape was built for writing software, where a failed attempt costs nothing and every change
            is reviewable as a diff before it ships.
          </p>
          <p class="text-lp-muted leading-relaxed">
            Business work is different. Actions are visible to clients and colleagues the moment they happen, many
            cannot be taken back, and nobody reviews them line by line afterward. So Collegium refuses four things
            autonomous frameworks celebrate, and each refusal is the point.
          </p>
        </div>
        <RefusalLedger />
        <p class="max-w-2xl leading-relaxed">
          And instead of autonomy, you get a system that runs your business logic as code. A procedure that matters is
          not a prompt instruction the model might skip on a bad day. It is a plugin: deterministic TypeScript you write
          once, review once, and grant to the agents that need it. It will not change under you.
        </p>
      </section>
    </main>
  </div>
</Layout>
